remediation:
1. **Replace vulnerable string concatenation with proper text methods:**
   - Use jQuery's `.text()` method instead of string concatenation for text content
   - The `.text()` method escapes HTML entities and prevents JavaScript execution

   ```javascript
   // VULNERABLE:
   resultsContainer.append('<h5>' + restaurant.name + '</h5>');

   // FIXED:
   var card = $('<div class="card mb-3"></div>');
   var body = $('<div class="card-body"></div>');
   $('<h5 class="card-title"></h5>').text(restaurant.name).appendTo(body);
   $('<p class="card-text"></p>').text(restaurant.description).appendTo(body);
   var smallText = $('<small class="text-muted"></small>').text(restaurant.category);
   $('<p class="card-text"></p>').append(smallText).appendTo(body);
   body.appendTo(card);
   resultsContainer.append(card);
   ```

2. **Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) headers:**
   - Add `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'` header
   - This prevents inline script execution and limits script sources to same-origin

3. **Use a templating engine with auto-escaping:**
   - Replace inline string concatenation with a templating library (Handlebars, Vue, React)
   - These libraries auto-escape user data by default

4. **Validate and sanitize all user input on the backend:**
   - Implement input validation for restaurant name, description, and category fields
   - Use a sanitization library like HTMLPurifier to remove any HTML/JavaScript from these fields

5. **Implement security code review process:**
   - Establish code review requirements for any JavaScript code that handles user data
   - Use automated security scanning tools (ESLint with security plugins, SonarQube) to detect XSS patterns

6. **Use a DOMPurify library if dynamic HTML is necessary:**
   - If HTML rendering is required, use DOMPurify to sanitize content: `resultsContainer.html(DOMPurify.sanitize(html))`

Priority: Apply fix #1 (replace with .text() method) immediately as it's the quickest mitigation.
